              ":question/id": "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-138",
              ":question/problem": "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186059863,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186059442,
              ":question/id": "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-139",
              ":question/problem": "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186059864,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186059443,
              ":question/id": "72830b06-6333-5b61-8257-3967312bd178",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-140",
              ":question/problem": "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = x^4 -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} + x$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - x^{-2} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} - (-2)x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186059865,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186059444,
              ":question/id": "acfa9025-f984-5b09-9e24-f72707ad67ac",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-141",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ for $y= 2x^3 + 6\\sqrt x$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we convert the radical into a rational exponent, and we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} + 6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3x^{(3 - 1)} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{(1/2 - 1)} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + 3 \\cdot x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"
          }
